  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe implement a multi-resolution simulated annealing with directed search (MSADS) approach to find a lower Erd\u0151s discrepancy constant C. Our strategy adapts the solution space by exponentially increasing the resolution while incorporating global exploration with simulated annealing and local refinement with gradient descent. The key innovation is an adaptive cooling schedule tied to the target improvement level, ensuring sufficient exploration until strong local candidates emerge.\n\nKey advantages:\n- Progressive resolution increase to escape local optima\n- Adaptive learning rate schedule for faster convergence\n- Hybrid global-local optimization combining simulated annealing and gradient-based refinement\n- Break condition triggered when improvement percentage drops below a threshold\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent the step function h(x) with n_points samples over [0,2]. The optimization problem:\n\nConstraints:\n- $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall i$\n- $\\sum h_i \\cdot dx = 1$\n\nObjective:\n- $C_5 = \\max_k \\text{np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[k] \\cdot dx$\n\nThe solution space is parameterized by real-valued vector h with the constraints enforced throughout optimization.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialize with parent solution if available (interpolated to minimal resolution)\nb. Periodically increase resolution exponentially (doubling every factor)\nc. Optimization loop combines:\n   - Simulated annealing with adaptive cooling based on loss improvement\n   - Gradient descent steps along the direction of steepest descent\n   - Memory of best previous solution at each level\nd. Candidate generation using:\n   - `generate_random_perturbation` with adaptive magnitude\n   - `gradient_descent_step` with learning rate decay\ne. Every 1000 steps, check if we should upgrade resolution when:\n   - Time set to expire\n   - Improvement percentage below 0.1% of current best\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Initial temperature: 10.0\n- Learning rate: starts at 0.08 and decays by 0.95 every 500 steps\n- Candidate acceptance criteria: EMA of recent improvement\n- Minimum improvement required for resolution upgrade: 0.05\n- Random perturbation scale: adaptive between 0.02 and 0.25\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nMajor costs: correlation computation (O(n log n) and gradient (O(n))\nTime allocation:\n- Global search: 60% of budget across resolutions\n- Local refinement: 40% of budget on best candidates\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nTerminal checks:\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points/2.0 within epsilon\n- C5 computed with np.correlate\nFall back to base solution if:\n- No valid solution found within budget\n- Constraint violation detected\n- Simulated annealing cycles many times without improvement",
